::Q1::What is fine tuning?
{ 
~Writing a longer prompt only
~Changing an API key
~Installing an IDE extension
=Further training a pre-trained model on a specialized dataset for a particular purpose
}

::Q2::When can fine tuning provide an advantage over prompting alone? 
{
~When the model should ignore all examples
~When the task is solved by one short prompt with no repeated pattern
=When a consistent specialized behavior is needed across many requests
~When no training examples exist
}

::Q3::Why evaluate a fine-tuned model? 
{
=To determine whether it performs the intended specialized task effectively
~Evaluation changes the model automatically
~Evaluation guarantees the model is unbiased
~Evaluation makes data unnecessary
}

::Q4::How does fine tuning differ from prompting? 
{
~Fine tuning is only a different spelling of prompting
~Both always change model weights
~Prompting permanently retrains the model
=Fine tuning changes the model through additional training, while prompting changes the input instructions
}

::Q5::What is a specialized support assistant an example of? 
{
~A database connection protocol
=A potential use case where fine tuning may help produce consistent domain-specific behavior
~A cloud storage bucket
~An EC2 instance type
}